#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158188} :activity/id #uuid "8762348d-01f7-5d75-ac1d-0c0a3228aa08" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049649 :topic/id #uuid "07f3ff28-90f4-5506-a524-0721208ea336" :topic/math-academy-id 2858} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158183 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157137 :question/id #uuid "64e8bf67-fc90-513e-a24c-1316fa483d22" :question/math-academy-id "e-7661"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354623}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "c3d1baf7-8ad6-5c85-acee-5848fe732ba3" :step/math-academy-id 28527 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158189}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157138 :question/id #uuid "bfde2074-5687-586c-af97-48d75b6b1724" :question/math-academy-id "e-7662"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049670}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354630}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2e4c8ad6-b6e4-5f83-848b-021c9125833d" :step/math-academy-id 28528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158188 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158511 :tutorial/content "When we have a logarithm in an inequality with base $n > 1,$ we can use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $n.$\n\nHowever, there is a catch: when we solve the resulting inequality, we must remember to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domain of the original logarithm.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\log_2 (x-1) < 4,\n$$\n\nwe write each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}(x-1)} & <{2}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <16 \\\\ x & <17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > 1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x < 17,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty, 17).$ But the domain of the logarithm is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $(1, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 17) \\cap (1, \\infty) = (1, 17)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (1,17),$ which can also be written as $1 < x < 17.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f8d2a28-784d-5ec0-b07f-a448d69b2d84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4665 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "142b0985-c893-5e26-8280-c0fd12dc1d25" :step/math-academy-id 28779 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158195}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158512 :tutorial/content "When we have two logarithms in an inequality, we can use the same solution procedure as before: we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of the base of the logarithm.\n\nJust remember that we still need to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domains of *all* the logarithms in the original inequality." :tutorial/id #uuid "f55c2fc8-9543-526f-8a35-04ec05516471" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4666 :tutorial/title "Inequalities with Two Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "c03fa6f8-ecd2-5532-ba6b-8afcdc3c4e6c" :step/math-academy-id 28780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158184}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158515 :tutorial/content "We're allowed to use each side of an inequality as the exponent of a number $n>1.$ But why are we allowed to do this?\n\nThe reason is that this operation preserves the ordering of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b$ and $n>1,$ then $n^a < n^b.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n-2 < -1 < 0 < 0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we raise $2$ to the power of these numbers, the results stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0.25}_{ 2^{-2} } < \\underbrace{0.5}_{ 2^{-1} } < \\underbrace{1}_{ 2^0 } < \\underbrace{\\,\\, 1.41\\ldots \\,\\,}_{ 2^{0.5} } < \\underbrace{2}_{ 2^1 } < \\underbrace{4}_{ 2^2 } < \\underbrace{8}_{ 2^3 }\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcec0706-6439-5e82-9c77-13f295dd1282" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4669 :tutorial/title "The Reason Why We Can Use Each Side as an Exponent"} :step/id #uuid "b51e214c-047d-565f-97e8-cdc1afe0b904" :step/math-academy-id 28788 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158183}}] :activity/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Logarithmic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157503}]}
